        -:    1:/* SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception */
        -:    2:/* cudapp-doctor: is this machine ready for CUDA++, and if not, what exactly to do.
        -:    3: *
        -:    4: * Developer bar D2 and D6 (docs/PAIN_POINTS.md). CUDA's install needs a driver, a toolkit, a
        -:    5: * framework build and an extension build whose versions all agree, and a mismatch shows up later
        -:    6: * as an undefined symbol or "no kernel image". CUDA++ has one thing to match: the NVIDIA kernel
        -:    7: * module its driver client speaks to. This program checks that and everything else a first run
        -:    8: * needs, and prints one line per check. Every failing line says what is wrong, why it matters and
        -:    9: * the command to run next.
        -:   10: *
        -:   11: * It reads files only (/proc, /sys, /dev): no device is opened and no GPU window is needed.
        -:   12: * CUDAPP_DOCTOR_ROOT=<dir> makes it read <dir>/proc, <dir>/sys and <dir>/dev instead (tests).
        -:   13: * Exit 0: READY. Exit 1: at least one FAIL line.
        -:   14: *
        -:   15: * Which lines to print, the module version parse, the memory figures and the FAIL count are the seeded
        -:   16: * Omega unit om/tools/doctor.om (omega_doctor). This file keeps the probes: fopen/fgets of the version
        -:   17: * file, access(2) of the device files and nvcc paths, the PCI scan and chip table, the meminfo sscanf
        -:   18: * and uname. -DCUDAPP_MIGRATION_PENDING_C_TOOLS_DOCTOR builds the former C body, kept as the oracle.
        -:   19: * Exit 2: the Omega unit refused (a build text holding % or a newline, a malformed chip refusal). */
